 "documents": {
  "attachments": [
   {
    "name": "ambulance_agreement.txt",
    "text": "EVENT MEDICAL SERVICES AGREEMENT\nAgreement no. AG-55068\n\nBetween: St. Luke Ambulance Brigade (\"the Provider\")\nAnd: Lantern Night Society (\"the Client\")\n\nEvent: Lantern City Race 7K\nEvent date: 2027-10-23\nLocation: Kingsway Gardens, Port Halden\n\nThe Provider will station one fully equipped ambulance with a crew of two paramedics on site at the top of Foundry Street from 08:30 until the last runner has finished or 12:00, whichever is later. The vehicle stays on site throughout and is dedicated to the event, except when transporting a patient from it.\n\nFee: $780, paid in full on signature.\n\nSigned for the Provider: Owen Cartwright, Operations Manager, 2027-07-17\nSigned for the Client: Samuel Adeyemi, 2027-07-17\n"
   },
   {
    "name": "crowd_plan.txt",
    "text": "Lantern City Race 7K - crowd notes\nSaturday 23 October 2027 / contact Samuel Adeyemi 555-0197\ntotal 800 incl 800 runners. We do not expect spectators in any number beyond the runners' own companions.\n\nVIEWING\nspectators wherever along course, mostly start + finish. stall near finish. signs to help ppl find their way\n\nSTART\n5 waves, 4 min gaps, seeded by expected time. pens close 5 min before each wave\n\nSTEWARDS\nclub vols + family helpers, event t-shirts. around all day\n\nEXIT\nrunners one-way funnel to recovery, out north gate. spectators 2 marked exits, steward on each. clear in 25 mins\n\nhired radio or 2 last time for start team, useful. short briefing for early arrivals. no complaints from runners\n\nCONTROL\nSamuel Adeyemi at control w/ radio to all supervisors. can hold wave, stop race, use PA. sups call in if area getting full\n"
   },
   {
    "name": "medical_plan.txt",
    "text": "Medical cover for the Lantern City Race 7K, Saturday 23 October 2027\n\nRunners get an email in race week telling them to dress for the weather and to keep a phone with them if they can. Most have run with us before. The list of entrants includes an emergency contact for each runner, which is kept at the start.\n\nFrom Samuel Adeyemi, Event Coordinator, Lantern Night Society\n\nThe race is a 7K with 800 runners and starts at 10:00. On-site ambulance cover is set out in the attached paperwork from St. Luke Ambulance Brigade.\n\nFirst aid\nWe'll have 3 first aid posts, at the start, the finish and halfway, with 6 trained first aiders between them, so nobody on the course is more than about a kilometre and a half from help. We also have two first aiders on bikes, carrying defibrillators.\n\nHeat, cold and water\nWe always tell runners to dress sensibly for the weather and to keep drinking, and it's all in the email we send them the week before. We'll be watching the forecast, and people here are used to all sorts of weather.\n\nIf someone needs hospital\nIf someone needed the hospital we'd dial 911 and the paramedics would take it from there. They know what they're doing far better than we do, and the city's service is very good.\n\nStaying in touch\nEveryone at a post and both bike first aiders has a radio on our medical channel, with phones as backup. Calls for an ambulance go through our medical lead, who tells the crew which barrier to come to.\n\nWho's responsible\nOur medical lead is Nadia Petrov, a registered paramedic, who'll be there from an hour before the start until the last runner is in and has the final word on pulling any runner out.\n"
   },
   {
    "name": "race_plan.txt",
    "text": "Lantern City Race 7K: how the race will work\n\nPrepared by Samuel Adeyemi (Event Coordinator), Lantern Night Society. Last updated 17 July 2027.\n\nThis is our plan for race day, Saturday 23 October 2027. We expect 800 runners over 7K (7 km). Numbers can be collected from the top of Foundry Street from an hour before we go at 10:00, and we aim to have the last finisher home by 11:30.\n\nThe route takes in Foundry Street, Weir Street and Ash Avenue, and the City would need to close all of these for us. On the way round we cross none. We finish in Exchange Square.\n\nThese are all big, wide streets. We measured Foundry Street at 16 metres and Ash Avenue at 10 metres, kerb to kerb, not counting the pavements. People watching have the pavement and the grass strip to spread out on.\n\nOur club has put on races for some years now and we keep to the same simple format: chip timing, water on the course, and a hot drink at the end. Results go up on our website the same evening.\n"
   },
   {
    "name": "traffic_plan.txt",
    "text": "Traffic arrangements for the Lantern City Race 7K, Saturday 23 October 2027\n\nFrom Samuel Adeyemi, Event Coordinator, Lantern Night Society\n\nWe have tried to think through what happens on the roads on race day. The race is a 7K, it starts at 10:00, and it goes through the Kingsway Gardens area. The police traffic unit has told us by phone that it will attend.\n\nClosing the roads\nWe need the following closures, and Pathfinder Road Crews will put out the barriers and signs for us. Foundry Street from Vine Street to Bridge Row, 08:30 until 12:00. Weir Street from Bridge Row to Dyer Street, 08:30 until 12:00. Ash Avenue from Vine Street to Rope Walk, 08:30 until 12:00.\n\nCrossings\nWe don't cross any major roads on this route, so there's nothing special to arrange here.\n\nGetting around the closures\nWe've worked out the way round for drivers and Pathfinder Road Crews will sign it. Traffic off Foundry Street goes via Prince Street and Tram Street, signed from Bridge Row. Traffic off Weir Street goes via High Street and Station Road, signed from Vine Street. Traffic off Ash Avenue goes via Ferry Road and Church Road, signed from Rope Walk.\n\nEmergency vehicles\nWe keep one lane clear the whole way, 3.5 metres or more, for ambulances and fire engines. Every barrier has someone with a radio on our channel, and they can have it open within two minutes. Our controller on the day has direct numbers for police and ambulance control.\n\nLetting people know\nEvery house and shop on the closed streets gets a letter three weeks before and a reminder a week before. We'll put up the yellow notice boards ten days ahead, and we've told the bus company the times.\n\nAfterwards\nOur sweep vehicle follows the last runner, and each closure comes off in turn once it has passed and the road is clear. Everything will be open again by 12:00.\n"
   }
  ],
  "cover_letter": "17 July 2027\n\nEvents Office\nCity of Port Halden\n\nDear Events Officer,\n\nI am writing on behalf of our organisation about this year's race.\n\nWe are applying for a street race permit for the Lantern City Race 7K on Saturday 23 October 2027. We expect 800 runners over 7K, starting at 10:00 in the Kingsway Gardens area. The completed form and the attachments (ambulance_agreement.txt, crowd_plan.txt, medical_plan.txt, race_plan.txt, traffic_plan.txt) are enclosed. The police have said they will look after the escort, though I haven't got anything in writing yet. We are fully insured for the event, as we are every year, so there's no concern there.\n\nPlease let me know if you need anything else.\n\nYours faithfully,\n\nSamuel Adeyemi\nEvent Coordinator, Lantern Night Society\nsamuel@lanternnight.example / 555-0197\n",
